5, -0.025726318359375, -0.00927734375, 0.053497314453125, 0.0281829833984375, 0.0236968994140625, 0.0125579833984375, 0.015777587890625, 0.00243377685546875, 0.010772705078125, -0.044219970703125, -0.00888824462890625, -0.0035037994384765625, -0.006153106689453125, 0.0275421142578125, -0.0282440185546875, 0.018707275390625, -0.018035888671875, -0.005611419677734375, 0.006855010986328125, 0.0036411285400390625, 0.011962890625, 0.0112762451171875, 0.00286865234375, 0.022064208984375, -0.01113128662109375, 0.019317626953125, -0.0113677978515625, 0.01192474365234375, -0.01517486572265625, 0.01934814453125, 0.01248931884765625, -0.00582122802734375, 0.0103912353515625, -0.01284027099609375, -0.023681640625, -0.0158538818359375, -0.01450347900390625, 0.0165557861328125, -0.04931640625, 0.0157928466796875, 0.007259368896484375, 0.01221466064453125, 0.0037326812744140625, -0.00534820556640625, -0.024932861328125, 0.01294708251953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave, and how they are perceived by higher-level players, are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. These models distinguish between two related but distinct constructs, which we define and operationalize separately:\n\n- **Level-0 actions (behavioral):** The actual choices made by players who do not engage in any explicit strategic reasoning about others\u2019 moves. In past literature, these are sometimes assumed to be random (i.e., uniform over the action space; see Nagel, 1995), or to reflect salient features such as midpoint or prominent numbers (e.g., Burchardi & Penczynski, 2014).\n- **Level-0 beliefs (expectational):** The explicit or elicited expectations by these players (or teams) regarding what other, similarly non-strategic (level-0) players would choose in the same context, under the assumption that those others are likewise uninformed by strategic considerations.\n\nBoth constructs are intended to capture non-strategic (\u201cna\u00efve\u201d) thought, but\u2014conceptually and empirically\u2014they can diverge. To clearly separate these constructs, we proceed in three steps: conceptually defining terms as above, specifying how we operationalize each in the lab, and then reporting empirical results and their interpretation.\n\n**Operationalization:**  \nIn our study, **level-0 actions** are defined as the selections made by participant teams after intra-team discussion. **Level-0 beliefs** are the teams' stated forecasts about what other level-0 actors (instructed to behave non-strategically) would choose in the same experimental setting. These were elicited in separate tasks, and both are classified at the team (not individual) level.\n\n**Methodology and Coding:**  \nWe employ the intra-team communication method of Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which aims to reveal underlying reasoning through within-team dialogue and written justifications. Teams of two participants (N_total = 25 teams: 14 teams in the beauty contest, 11 in the 11-20 game; 50 subjects in total) jointly select an action, providing a written summary of their reasoning before the choice. To classify teams as demonstrating level-0 reasoning, trained coders used an explicit rubric (see Appendix A) adapted from Burchardi & Penczynski and Goeree et al. (2018). Key criteria include:\n\n- *Absence of reasoning about others' likely strategies or iterative thinking* (e.g., \u201cNo mention of what others will do, or adjustment in response to anticipated choices.\u201d)\n- *Reliance on salience or payoff structure without strategic inference*, for example:\n    - Salient feature: \u201c50 is right in the middle,\u201d or \u201c20 is the highest possible number.\u201d\n    - Payoff structure: \u201cChoosing 20 gets the biggest reward, with the possibility of a bonus.\u201d\n    - Focality: \u201cThis number stands out.\u201d\n- *No evidence of statistical, probabilistic, or simulated best response thinking.*\n\nCoders were trained to distinguish between references to game features (salience, payoff maxima, endpoints) and implicit or explicit strategic reasoning (\u201cIf others choose X, then\u2026\u201d or \u201cBecause most people will...\u201d are not level-0). In ambiguous cases, coders consulted the protocol (see Appendix A for excerpts) and discussed until consensus. The final percent agreement was 92%, with Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.88, reflecting high inter-rater reliability. Table 1 presents anonymized sample justifications by coding category, illustrating typical level-0 reasoning.\n\n**Experimental Contexts:**  \nThe study was conducted in two standard settings:\n\n1. *Beauty contest* (Burchardi & Penczynski, 2014): Players choose a number from 0\u2013100, goal being two-thirds of the group average. Prior work (Nagel, 1995; Burchardi & Penczynski, 2014) finds level-0 actions often cluster at the midpoint (e.g., 50), reflecting salience or centrality.\n2. *11-20 game* (Goeree, Holt, & Palfrey, 2018): Players select an integer 11\u201320, earning that number in base payoff and a bonus for being uniquely lowest. Previous studies (Goeree et al., 2018, Table 3; Crawford, Costa-Gomes & Iriberri, 2013) show level-0 behavior can cluster at salient \u201csafe\u201d values, not at random or the minimum.\n\n**Quantitative Results:**  \nIn the beauty contest, among the 14 teams classified as level-0:\n\n- Mean action: 51.2 (SD = 7.1, 95% CI [46.7, 55.7])\n- Mean level-0 belief: 49.8 (SD = 7.9, 95% CI [44.7, 54.9])\n- Only one team selected a value deviating by more than 10 from the midpoint, consistent with clustering at central salient values.\n- Table 1 Panel A: Shows representative reasoning (e.g., \u201cThe number 50 is in the middle and stands out.\u201d)\n\nIn the 11-20 game, for 11 level-0 teams:\n\n- Mean action: 18.7 (SD = 1.6, 95% CI [17.8, 19.6]); modal action 20 (5 teams); 0 teams selected 11; remaining choices in 17\u201319.\n- Level-0 beliefs: Mean = 19.1 (SD = 1.3, 95% CI [18.3, 19.9]), similarly right-skewed.\n- Table 1 Panel B: Shows \u201c20 is safest\u201d or \u201cThe highest number avoids losing the bonus\u201d as typical justifications.\n\nIn both games, the divergence between action and belief was rare. In 2/25 sessions (8%), there was a difference of >6 units; post-hoc coding (see Table 1 Panel C) suggests these cases involved emphasis on personal risk (for action) versus group conservatism (for belief). No statistically meaningful systematic difference between actions and beliefs was observed (paired t-test, p > 0.1).\n\n*Figures and Tables:*  \n- **Figure 1** presents histograms of team-level actions and beliefs for both games:\n    - *Panel A*: Beauty contest responses cluster sharply at ~50.\n    - *Panel B*: 11-20 game responses cluster near 20, with clear absence of minimum values.\n    - *Panel C*: Cases of within-team action-belief divergence are labeled, underscoring their rarity.\n\n**Interpretation and Context:**  \nOur intra-team elicitation extends Goeree et al. (2018) and Burchardi & Penczynski (2014) by showing that teams classified as level-0 systematically anchor on payoff-salient or \u201csafe\u201d values (endpoints, maxima, midpoints), and explicitly reference risk and coordination incentives\u2014not mere randomization or uniform central tendency. This supports Crawford et al.\u2019s (2013) critique that context-sensitive features, not acontextual randomization, underpin \u201cnon-strategic\u201d play.\n\n**Limitations and Generalizability:**  \nSeveral limitations are noteworthy:\n\n- *Intra-team dynamics:* Group discussion may induce convergence or groupthink (Janis, 1982), possibly suppressing minority views or idiosyncratic approaches, and limiting detection of within-team heterogeneity or partial strategic awareness. This may affect generalizability to individual settings.\n- *Summary-based coding:* Written justifications may omit implicit strategy or nonverbal reasoning, potentially biasing coding outcomes.\n- *Sample size and design:* The study involves a relatively modest number of sessions (N = 25 teams) and covers only two games. Population composition (students in a laboratory context) and prior exposure to such games (not systematically controlled) may also influence what counts as \u201csalient.\u201d\n- *External validity:* The exclusive use of intra-team elicitation may not capture the full range of level-0 heterogeneity or reflect behavior in field or online contexts.\n\nAddressing these, future work should counterbalance individual and team tasks, randomly assign to different elicitation modes, and include direct measures of prior game exposure and manipulation checks for strategic awareness. Techniques such as process tracing and individual think-aloud protocols may further uncover subtle forms of reasoning.\n\n**Terminology and Attribution:**  \nThroughout, we use *level-0* to refer specifically to non-strategic, non-iterative reasoning, operationally defined and empirically identified as above. When referencing \u201cna\u00efve,\u201d \u201cnon-strategic,\u201d or \u201crandom,\u201d we clarify context or modeling assumption. Empirical clustering at salient values in beauty contest and 11-20 games is established in Burchardi & Penczynski (2014, Fig. 2) and Goeree et al. (2018, Table 3); critiques of fixed/\u201crandom\u201d level-0 types are articulated by Crawford et al. (2013, Sec. 2.2).\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nOur evidence demonstrates that both level-0 actions and beliefs are flexibly and systematically shaped by the payoff structure and salient features of the experimental context, with rare but interpretable action-belief divergence. For theorists and experimentalists, this implies level-0 types should be constructed using operational criteria that combine payoff salience, focal values, and risk avoidance (as in Burchardi & Penczynski, 2014, and Crawford et al., 2013), not simple randomization or midpoint heuristics. Modeling approaches may include empirically estimating level-0 response rules from observed \u201cnon-strategic\u201d play or parameterizing level-0 types as explicit functions of game structure and coordination incentives. Future research should systematically compare individual and team-based elicitations to test for"}}